# recovery/BIN_PARITY_EXEMPT -- targets exempt from the bin-parity byte gate.
#
# `make -C recovery bin-parity` clean-rebuilds every committed
# recovery/bin/<arch>/ artifact with the pinned toolchain (recovery/TOOLCHAIN)
# and SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH and byte-compares it against the committed file.  A
# target listed here is NOT byte-compared -- because its current toolchain
# produces non-byte-reproducible output -- but bin-parity STILL clean-rebuilds
# it (proving it compiles+links) and prints the exemption loudly.
#
# Exemption is a liability, not a free pass: GATE-08 step 5 mandates that each
# exempt target carry a tracking issue.  Drop a target the moment its toolchain
# yields byte-reproducible output (e.g. a future zig with deterministic
# Mach-O/PE linking, or a documented post-link normalization step).
#
# Format (one per line):  <rel-path-under-bin/>  issue=#N  reason="..."
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored.
#
#
# EMPTY as of the #320 promotion: link-time stripping made both former
# exemption classes byte-reproducible, so ALL committed targets are now
# byte-gated.
#   - Mach-O: zig 0.16.0 recomputed LC_UUID + chained-fixup/function-starts/
#     code-directory hashes per run.  LC_UUID is a content hash; with debug
#     stripped (-Wl,-S) the content is stable and back-to-back builds are
#     byte-identical.
#   - PE: the per-run debug-directory checksum lived in the stripped
#     sections; with -Wl,--strip-debug back-to-back builds are
#     byte-identical.
# If a future toolchain regresses, re-add the affected targets here WITH a
# fresh tracking issue.
